    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1375">

                <pb facs="1477" n="1463" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1463"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">103.9169/9–3044: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Paraguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Beaulac</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Asunción</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-09-30T08:45:00-04:00">September 30,
                            1944—8:45 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 5—5 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–318. For Crowley and Lebensburger,<anchor n="21" xml:id="d1375-f1"/> FEA.
                    Reference Department’s circular airgram dated September 16, 1944, 12 p.m.<anchor
                        n="22" xml:id="d1375-f2"/> concerning simplification Import Recommendation
                    Procedure. Both Embassy and Paraguayan Country Agency fully in accord with
                    suggested procedure, which if followed according to the text of the proposal,
                    would appear to eliminate considerable detail for all concerned, without
                    detracting in any way from the essentiality and value of the Import
                    Recommendation.</p>
                <p>If and when the suggested procedure becomes effective, the Paraguayan Country
                    Agency desires to reserve the right to continue sending one copy of the Import
                    Recommendation to the Paraguayan Embassy at Washington, D.C. in order that that
                    office may be kept fully informed regarding pending applications and thus be in
                    a position to offer a prospective applicant or exporter such assistance as the
                    Embassy there may be in a position to render, if called upon. Local Country
                    Agency has also indicated its desire to continue use of present Import
                    Recommendation forms, of which it has a substantial quantity on hand.</p>
                <p>It is assumed that Import Recommendations will continue to be required only for
                    commodities as designated on the Positive List.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beaulac</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1375-f1" n="21" xml:id="d1375-f1-content">
                <p>S. H. Lebensburger, Director, Requirements and Supply Branch, Bureau of Supplies,
                    Foreign Economic Administration.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1375-f2" n="22" xml:id="d1375-f2-content">
